@vsm 1.0
// beacon_activation.spec.vsm — activate the VSF beacon as the network-level EVC gate.
// author: Claude/Fable [O] design 2026-07-03 → DS [S] impl. rmw3 (S5) ratifies the protocol.
// context: vsf-api (n01:8080) is the VSF beacon (covenant/handshake/registry) but DORMANT
// (registry.jsonl never written — 0 kernels ever registered) and INSECURE (POST /registry unauth,
// plain HTTP, no systemd). "Activate it" = wire the nuclei to beacon in, AUTHENTICATED.

spec(BEACON-ACTIVATION) {

  insight:
    "The beacon is the NETWORK-LEVEL manifestation of 'calibrate-before-trust'. Axis-1 calibrated every
     nucleus instrument LOCALLY (2026-07-03). The beacon is where a node PROVES — to the network — that it
     is covenant-compliant AND its instruments are calibrated, before it is trusted to participate. Same
     principle as Axis-1 + the EVC (embedded viability certifier), one recursion level up."

  current_state:
    "GET /covenant (public ✓) + GET /handshake (Phase-0 ψ coupling invitation ✓) + POST /registry
     (register_kernel → registry.jsonl, UNAUTH, spoofable). Dormant since Apr; the nucleus never beacons."

  // ── THE ATTESTATION PROTOCOL (the fix) ────────────────────────────────────────
  attestation_protocol: {
    trust_root: "the OFFLINE S5 CA (ed25519 root 624e8d3e, WSL+USB; memory project_s5_ca). It issues node
                 CERTS during provisioning; the beacon PINS the S5-CA root pubkey. Standard PKI: root offline,
                 nodes sign with their issued cert key, beacon verifies against the pinned root.",
    kernel_birth_payload: {
      kernel_id:    "the node id (e.g. vsf_nucleus, s1-clima-…)",
      vsf_version:  "binary sha / version",
      watermark:    "⟦ {node-id} | rmw3 | vsm-x.y | vsf.u2738.org ⟧",
      covenant_sha: "sha256 of the covenant the node attests to (must match the beacon's current covenant)",
      calibration:  "hash of the node's PASSING calibration census (the Axis-1 proof: all instruments
                     discriminate) — the node attests its gauges are calibrated, not just that it exists",
      cert:         "the node's S5-CA-issued certificate (chains to the pinned root)",
      sig:          "ed25519 signature over the canonicalized payload, with the node's cert key",
    },
    beacon_verify: [
      "1. cert chains to the pinned S5-CA root (else REJECT).",
      "2. sig verifies over the payload with the cert key (else REJECT).",
      "3. covenant_sha == the beacon's current covenant sha (else REJECT — stale/forked covenant).",
      "4. (optional, phase 2) the calibration hash matches a re-attestable census (EVC deep check).",
      "→ PASS: record a TRUSTED registration {node, ts, ip, version, covenant_sha, calibration}.",
      "→ FAIL: record a REJECTED attempt (this is the real value the honeypot-style log was reaching for) + algedonic.",
    ]
  }

  wiring: {
    nucleus: "on kernel_birth (startup), the nucleus builds the payload above (it already has the covenant
              sha + the calibration census; sign with its S5-CA cert) and POSTs it to the beacon. Retry/backoff;
              non-fatal if the beacon is unreachable (beaconing is announce-only, not a boot gate).",
    others:  "same for each S1 kernel — the SAME signed-beacon routine (recursive; reuse a shared client).",
  }

  hardening: {
    tls:     "put the beacon behind Caddy like the bastion: a route (api.u2738.org OR vsf.u2738.org/beacon)
              → reverse_proxy localhost:PORT, with the SAME security headers (HSTS/CSP/X-Frame DENY).",
    bind:    "rebind uvicorn to 127.0.0.1 (not 0.0.0.0); Caddy is the only public ingress.",
    systemd: "make it a managed unit (auto-restart, journald). No more manual/nohup.",
  }

  acceptance:
    "the nucleus registers on birth with a valid signed attestation; registry.jsonl records it as TRUSTED;
     an UNSIGNED/invalid POST is REJECTED + logged; the beacon is behind Caddy (TLS), localhost-bound, systemd-managed.
     Then the beacon is ACTIVE and the network onboarding is authenticated."

  // ── CONTINUOUS CONTROL (rmw3 2026-07-03) — a peripheral that failed silently must be MONITORED ──
  continuous_control:
    "The beacon is a PERIPHERAL extension (an external appendage, S1-like), and it FAILED SILENTLY — dormant
     since April, discovered only by accident during the security assessment. A viable system does not let a
     peripheral die unnoticed. REQUIRED:
     (1) DECLARE — register the beacon as a peripheral component in state.db (+ coupling_registry) so the
         model≡reality census knows it EXISTS (today it is a manual n01 process, invisible to the census).
     (2) CONTINUOUS HEALTH PROBE — a timer (pattern: vsf-topology-reconcile) probes the beacon END-TO-END:
         port alive + GET /health 200 + FUNCTIONAL (reachable from OUTSIDE its host; registry writable). Not
         just process-up — the Valparaíso/forecaster lesson: EXERCISE it, do not assume.
     (3) ALGEDONIC ON FAILURE — probe fails N times → emit a beacon_down algedonic signal (sev high) to the
         S5 ledger. A dead peripheral must raise PAIN, not silence.
     (4) REDUNDANT CHANNELS (future) — a single HTTP beacon is a SPOF. Add a fallback: a NATS-based
         registration path (a node registers via NATS if HTTP is down) OR a mirror beacon. Registration
         should not depend on a single channel."

  general_principle:
    "The beacon's silent death exposed a MONITORING BLIND SPOT: the S3* liveness census covers the nucleus +
     DECLARED LXCs on n02, but NOT peripherals / manual processes / n01 appendages. GENERAL FIX: every
     component — core AND peripheral — must be DECLARED + HEALTH-PROBED + algedonic-on-failure. A component
     that can die silently is a viability blind spot. Extends RECON-BIDIRECTIONAL (running→declared) to the
     peripheral/n01 side. → task(PERIPHERAL-HEALTH-CENSUS): enumerate ALL peripherals (beacon, honeypot,
     caddy, vsf-bastion, any n01/manual process), declare each, add a health probe + algedonic per one."

  owner: "Claude/Fable [O] (this design + the payload/verify protocol + the monitoring) → DS [S] (impl beacon
          verify + nucleus wiring + Caddy/systemd + the health probe + declare). rmw3 (S5): ratify the protocol
          + confirm the S5-CA cert issuance for nodes."
  bridges: "S5 CA (project_s5_ca) · witness-verify/AX-STATE · covenant · EVC (project_embedded_viability_certifier)
            · the Axis-1 calibration census (the attestation content)."
  cert_issuance:  // the one [rmw3] step (offline S5 CA on WSL+USB):
    "rmw3 issues the nucleus a node cert from the offline S5 CA (root 624e8d3e). DS builds + tests the whole
     flow on LXC 108 with a TEST cert (a throwaway CA), then the REAL nucleus cert is issued + installed for
     prod. Beacon pins the real S5-CA root pubkey. Later nodes (S1s) get certs the same way at onboarding."

  ca_recursion_ready:  // rmw3 APPROVED 2026-07-04 — cheap insurance for scale:
    "design the CA so it can DELEGATE down recursion levels (each level's CA issues to its children), even though
     we use ONE level now. A FLAT CA would need rebuilding when the framework scales (scaling_trajectory.vsm) —
     a hierarchical-capable structure is the same ceremony, no extra cost now. Applies to BOTH the beacon node
     certs AND the S5 operational decision-key (decision_auth_fix.spec.vsm): ONE PKI, recursion-ready from day one."
  status: "RATIFIED 2026-07-03 — attestation protocol locked. → DS [S] impl (beacon verify + nucleus signing +
           Caddy/systemd + health probe + declare); build/test on 108 with a test cert; rmw3 issues the real
           nucleus cert for prod cutover."
}
